<section id="faq" style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Questions devs actually ask.</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details open><summary>Does it work on a Claude Max plan?</summary><p>Yes. On a flat plan you don’t save money, you save quota: grunt work stops eating your Opus limits, so the plan lasts longer.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Will it slow me down?</summary><p>Routing decisions run on your machine, and we will publish measured latency before launch. If Horsy fails or is slow, everything runs as before. It never blocks you.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What if it routes something wrong?</summary><p>The check catches it and the task steps up one rung, with the reason. If a task type keeps losing, Horsy turns itself off for that type. You can also pin a model per task, or run <code>/horsy off</code>.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does my code leave my machine?</summary><p>Your code never goes to Horsy’s servers; routing data is metadata only (task type, model, cost, pass or fail). The one exception is a remote judge for tasks without tests, which you choose in <code>horsy.yaml</code>: it sees the snippet it checks.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it beat just using Sonnet for everything?</summary><p>We don’t know yet, and we won’t claim it until we’ve tested it on real coding sessions. The receipts will show it either way, including when the number is negative.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is this different from a model router?</summary><p>Routers pick a model before the task and stop there. Horsy also checks the work before accepting “done”, steps up a rung when the check fails, writes a receipt per step with the net cost, and turns itself off for task types where it loses.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
